WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.090
Years can go by between the announcement of
a big Hollywood film project and its actual

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.020
arrival in theaters.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.600
But sometimes studios and distributors will
shelf a finished film for years if they believe

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.280
it's in their best financial or promotional
interest.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.420
Here are a few films that took years to appear
on the big screen.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:17.880
Red Dawn

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.740
Turning to remakes of popular films from its
vaults in the late 2000s to help stave off

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:28.940
financial ruin, MGM hired Dan Bradley to helm
a remake of the 1984 Cold War film Red Dawn.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.520
It's about a ragtag group of teenagers who
mount a resistance against invading Soviet

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:34.240
Union forces.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.460
"I know we're outnumbered and outgunned.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.800
But that doesn't mean we can't make a difference."

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.150
The remake starring Chris Hemsworth and set
in the present day, originally had Chinese

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:44.650
antagonists.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.220
But to quell controversy and increase the
chances of a Chinese release, the villains

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.920
were changed to the North Koreans in post-production.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:56.860
The updated Red Dawn was originally scheduled
for release in 2010, but was shelved due to

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.960
the studio's then-pending bankruptcy.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:04.799
While it was in limbo, the filmmakers spent
less than $1 million in edits, CGI, and dubbing

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:06.990
to remove all-things-China from the film.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:13.009
It was finally released on November 23, 2012
... but never in China, despite the work done

00:01:13.009 --> 00:01:14.740
to make the film more China-friendly.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.280
The Cabin in the Woods

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.900
The satirical horror gem The Cabin in the
Woods, co-written and produced by Joss Whedon,

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:27.219
is another Chris Hemsworth-led film that was
tangled up in MGM's disastrous financial crisis

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:28.160
in the late 2000s.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.359
"Their ears are stopped."

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:33.680
"They are the Gods' fools."

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.220
"Well ... that's how it works."

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.479
Originally scheduled for release on February
5, 2010, the film didn't arrive in theaters

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.300
until more than two years later.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.040
After initially delaying the film so they
could convert it to 3D, MGM shelved the movie

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:50.800
indefinitely after succumbing to its financial
woes.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:55.420
Instead of pushing forward with a new distribution
strategy, newly appointed executives chose

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:00.299
to sell the movie, along with Red Dawn, to
Lionsgate in 2011, and it finally made its

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.520
debut on April 13th, 2012.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.080
Repo Men

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:10.040
Based on the novel Repossession Mambo by Eric
Garcia, 2010's Repo Men stars Jude Law and

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:14.920
Forest Whitaker as agents for a company called
The Union that sells bio-mechanical organs.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.420
When the company's customers fail to pay their
due, The Union sends agents to "repossess"

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:20.920
the goods.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:23.700
"Don't you touch me."

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:25.460
"No need for violence, miss."

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:35.220
The film entered production in 2007 — two
years prior to Garcia's novel being published

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.489
— and finished filming the following year.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:41.560
However, since another film with a very similar
title and premise, Repo!

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.120
The Genetic Opera, was also in the pipeline,
Universal chose to shelve the film to avoid

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.460
competition, not releasing it for another
two years.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.560
House of 1000 Corpses

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.060
Rob Zombie's cult horror classic House of
1000 Corpses took a long and arduous road

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:56.560
to theaters.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.480
Originally filmed in 2000, the movie followed
the Firefly family as they travelled across

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.460
the country on Halloween, killing and maiming
along the way.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:11.800
Due to the amount of blood, gore, and violence,
the film received an NC-17 rating by the MPAA,

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.840
prompting Universal to deem the film a bad
investment and shelve it indefinitely.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.960
Several months later, Zombie purchased the
rights from Universal and began searching

00:03:19.969 --> 00:03:24.459
for another distributor, with MGM eventually
picking up the film and scheduling it for

00:03:24.460 --> 00:03:26.150
release in October 2002.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.379
However, MGM later dropped the project after
Zombie reportedly joked that the studio had

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.660
"no morals" since they chose to distribute
it.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.800
Lionsgate ultimately released a toned-down
R-rated cut of the flick on April 11th, 2003.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:41.660
Take Me Home Tonight

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.840
The 2011 retro comedy Take Me Home Tonight
arguably came closer than most films at capturing

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.769
the essence of the '80s — but that's one
of the few positive things critics had to

00:03:51.769 --> 00:03:52.600
say about the film.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.719
Set in Los Angeles in 1988, the comedy follows
Topher Grace's

00:03:56.719 --> 00:04:01.269
Matt Franklin, as he pursues his high school
crush and reconnects with old friends over

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:02.739
the course of one wild night.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.239
"What do you do, anyway?

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.280
I thought you went to MIT."

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.440
"I work at Suncoast Video."

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.790
Since the movie featured heavy use of cocaine
as part of the night's crazy hijinks, Universal

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:17.160
didn't know how to go about marketing it,
which led to the studio indefinitely shelving

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:21.140
the film until Rogue acquired its distribution
rights for $10 million dollars.

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:26.860
Take Me Home Tonight finally debuted on March
4, 2011, four years after it finished filming.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:27.920
"Wow.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:30.360
Congratulations?"

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:31.740
Thanks for watching!

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:34.300
